{"reference":"2 Corinthians 1","book":"2 Corinthians","chapter":1,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"historical_context","title":"Opening and authorship","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"The letter opens by naming Paul and Timothy as its senders to the Corinthian assembly and believers throughout Achaia. Pauline authorship of the material in canonical 2 Corinthians has strong critical support, although questions about how many earlier letter units the canonical form may preserve remain disputed.","verse_refs":["2 Corinthians 1:1","2 Corinthians 1:1-2"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_corinthians_001_5136ef34f4afb1db7a775c6d"]}]},{"kind":"chapter_overview","title":"Comfort shared in affliction","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"Verses 3–11 connect affliction, comfort, endurance, prayer, thanksgiving, and deliverance. Comfort is not presented as a claim that suffering itself is desirable; it is received amid affliction and becomes a means of supporting others. Paul also describes being pressed beyond strength, despairing of life, and feeling a sentence of death, while leaving the precise event unidentified.","verse_refs":["2 Corinthians 1:3-11","2 Corinthians 1:8-10"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_corinthians_001_7a0a2c36a3fb5e42122629c2"]}]},{"kind":"why_it_matters","title":"Honest need and shared care","blocks":[{"id":"block_3","text":"A disputed contemporary reading sees this passage as making room for candid acknowledgement of despair or crisis without treating it as failed faith. It centers care for others and mutual reliance, rather than making suffering a credential or demanding a display of recovery.","verse_refs":["2 Corinthians 1:3-11","2 Corinthians 1:8-10"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_corinthians_001_c1cf20ddff09725b655add2f"]},{"id":"block_4","text":"Paul joins deliverance with the Corinthians’ prayers and with thanksgiving offered by many. In a disputed contemporary extension, shared prayer does not make failed recovery proof of inadequate prayer or hidden sin, and it is not a substitute for urgent safety assessment, material aid, appropriate reporting or investigation, or medical and mental-health care when these are needed.","verse_refs":["2 Corinthians 1:10-11","2 Corinthians 1:8-11"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_corinthians_001_f8237df619277d74f5e8fd7d"]}]},{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Changed plans and limited authority","blocks":[{"id":"block_5","text":"Paul explains that he had intended a double visit, denies acting carelessly, and says that he delayed coming in order to spare the Corinthians. His defense makes changed plans and damaged trust discussable; it does not make every leader’s intention beyond question. The chapter itself closes by limiting apostolic authority: Paul and his coworkers are not to rule over the Corinthians’ faith, but to work with them for joy.","verse_refs":["2 Corinthians 1:15-22","2 Corinthians 1:23-24"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_corinthians_001_97421dda0522e89f3ed30aa4"]}]},{"kind":"why_it_matters","title":"Authority accountable to others","blocks":[{"id":"block_6","text":"A disputed contemporary extension of Paul’s refusal to “lord it over” faith rejects founder domination and apostolic immunity. It understands accountable leadership to require local decision-making, respect for language and culture, transparent finances, channels for complaints, independent review, and protection from retaliation.","verse_refs":["2 Corinthians 1:23-24"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_corinthians_001_230a3e6782c6fd452cedf3d9"]}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"Letter and travel setting","blocks":[{"id":"block_7","text":"2 Corinthians is commonly dated to the mid-50s CE, after 1 Corinthians and before Romans. The later discussion of travel helps explain why the itinerary in this chapter needs caution: the related accounts do not provide a complete shared calendar for reconstructing every movement and visit.","verse_refs":["2 Corinthians 2:1-13","2 Corinthians 2:1-4"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_corinthians_001_a78fc5e23b0fe601775b0a07","reader_path_2_corinthians_001_500993f22c6cd26fe4deb8d6"]}]}],"generated_metadata":null}